WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.240
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.280
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:23.540
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:30.720
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myth and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.520
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.080
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:51.760
The curiosities I find particularly fascinating.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.920
The bodies of some animals stretch and shrink in extraordinary ways.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.800
Constrictor snakes can swallow prey twice their own size.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.640
While the camel's hump can almost double in weight, giving it the energy to travel

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.360
huge distances across deserts.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:27.680
What is the secret behind such expandable bodies?

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.720
We've long been fascinated by the camel's ability to live in the harshest of deserts.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:40.120
Places where during summer temperatures can soar up to 50 degrees Celsius.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:47.080
While in winter they can drop to 30 degrees below freezing.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:52.180
With little in the way of food or water, camels can sometimes go without eating or drinking

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.240
for over a week.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.400
Most other animals couldn't survive conditions like this.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.480
How does the camel do it?

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.640
The camel's secret was thought to lie in its hump.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.520
In a healthy camel it can be big and firm like this one.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:18.560
And can weigh as much as 30 kilos, which is the weight of a 10 year old child.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:24.120
But if the camel goes without food and particularly water for any length of time, then the hump

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.960
can get floppy and even droop over on one side as that one has done.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:39.600
So people used to think that the camel stored water in its hump.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.540
In fact there are two different kinds of camel.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:49.320
The one humped or dromedary and the two humped or bachshund.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:57.880
Nearly all camels alive today are the domesticated descendants of one or the other.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.560
The wild dromedary almost certainly doesn't exist.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.320
And only a few bachshund camels remain roaming the deserts of Central Asia.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:17.760
The camel is a very tough animal, but in the wild today it's rarer than the giant panda.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.300
It's hard to say where the idea of a water storing hump came from.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:30.000
The ancient Romans were the first to suggest that the camel may have a built in water reservoir.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.880
And then later on people got the idea that it had two stomachs, one for food and one

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.740
for water.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:44.280
In the 18th century an eminent anatomist John Hunter decided to investigate the truth behind

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.160
these assertions and he dissected a camel.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.360
He found that the stomach consisted of three or four compartments similar to those of a

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.040
cow or a sheep.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:01.200
But inside one of those compartments he discovered these pocket-like structures which are not

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.840
found in any other large mammal.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:09.160
Hunter didn't know what the pockets were for, but others after him proposed that they

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.360
were special water storage cells.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.920
And then despite any kind of evidence to prove that this was true, for another 250 years

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.880
books on natural history like this one featured illustrations of water storage cells in the

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.240
camel's stomach.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:34.600
We now know that that's not true, even though we don't know exactly what the strange pockets

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.560
are for.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.920
But the camel's hump is certainly not filled with water.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.000
It's made entirely of fatty tissue.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.380
It is in fact an energy reserve for times when food is scarce and it can expand to such

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.840
a degree that it makes up 80% of the camel's body fat.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.940
This enables a camel to go for two weeks without feeding if necessary.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:06.460
But there's a twist to the story.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:12.960
When fat is broken down in the body it produces not just energy but also water.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.200
In fact each gram of fat broken down during metabolism produces one gram of water.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.760
So could the camel's hump provide it with extra water after all?

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.240
A fatty hump that contains both food and water would seem to be just what a desert animal

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.240
needs.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.320
But it's not as simple as that.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:41.900
To consume its fat an animal needs more oxygen so it has to breathe more.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:46.760
So when living on the fat in its hump the camel actually loses more water through its

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.760
airways than it gains.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.000
So the camel doesn't have a secret store of water.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.240
How then can it survive in a waterless desert?

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.880
Camels can grow without drinking for more than a week because they have an extraordinary

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:07.880
ability to retain the body moisture.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:13.880
We ourselves lose over a litre of water a day through our moisture laden breath.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:18.380
But the camel has nostrils which you can shut tight.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:24.000
And that not only keeps out the sand but retains the breath within the nose.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:33.040
And there the moisture can be reabsorbed by the linings of the nostrils.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:40.360
Most mammals also lose a lot of water when they cool their bodies by sweating.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:47.220
But camels can endure a rise in body temperature that would kill most other mammals without

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.520
sweating.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:57.200
If our temperature goes up by as little as one degree it's a sign of illness.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:04.440
While three degrees causes vital organ damage and eventually death.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:11.960
The camel can cope with as much as six degrees rise with no ill effect.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:18.280
This means that camels don't have to sweat until conditions get very hot indeed.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:26.360
And if necessary they tolerate losing more of their body water than other mammals.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:31.320
When animals become dehydrated their blood becomes thicker and more difficult to pump

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.720
through the body.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:38.180
If we lose ten percent of our body water we start to go dizzy and blind and at fifteen

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:42.560
percent our internal organs start to fail.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:46.840
Camels however can lose a third of their body water with no ill effect.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.520
Something that would kill most other animals.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.400
How do they do it?

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.360
Well some of the answers may lie in the shape of their blood cells.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.240
These are the red blood cells from a human being which are disc shaped like that of most

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.320
mammals.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:12.120
These on the other hand are from a camel and are slimmer and more oval in shape.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:18.360
It may be that the oval streamlined shape makes it easier for the blood to flow when

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.320
the animal is dehydrated.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.240
Unfortunately a camel's blood is less thick and sticky than ours.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.760
The cells also have particularly strong walls.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:37.200
This prevents them from rupturing when the animal suddenly drinks large amounts of water

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:43.960
and when they do find water camels have the ability to drink it very quickly.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:48.960
A single camel can take the contents of all these bottles, that's a hundred litres, in

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.040
a mere ten minutes.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:54.760
For any other animal to do that it would be extremely dangerous.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:59.760
But the camel has the ability to hold the water in the stomach and only release it into

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:07.240
the bloodstream very slowly in a way that does no damage.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:13.680
We now understand how camels can survive harsh desert conditions and yet surprisingly new

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:21.320
research suggests that they may first have evolved to live in the cold Arctic.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:27.640
The scientists have recently discovered the fossil bones of giant shaggy camels that roamed

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:34.280
the forests of the Canadian Arctic some three and a half million years ago.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:39.160
The Arctic camel was a third larger than the modern Bactrian but otherwise looked very

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:44.360
similar and that may be no coincidence.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.560
The wide flat feet that stopped the camel from sinking into desert sand could also have

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:56.880
helped its ancestors walk in deep snow and a fatty hump provided the food reserve a camel

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:02.160
would need to survive long cold winters.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:08.160
We may never fully understand the mysteries of the camel's hump whether it evolved first

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:14.920
as a way of keeping warm or staying cool but we have unraveled many other mysteries of

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:20.320
the animal's body that enable it to endure conditions that few other animals would be

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.340
able to withstand.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:30.960
The camel's hump can change dramatically in shape and size and in part two we meet another

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:45.520
creature that can stretch its body in the most extraordinary way.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.960
The camel can go for days without water.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:56.280
Our second curiosity can fast for months on end and then devour prey many times its own

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.720
size.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:05.240
This is a green anaconda one of the largest snakes in the world.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:11.200
It's about four meters long and weighs 70 kilos and it's only half grown.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:17.240
They can go to a length of six meters and weigh twice as much as this one but it's their

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:24.100
ability to be able to swallow enormous prey that's really grabbed our imagination.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:32.240
Could one of these really bite a man and swallow him whole and alive?

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:38.160
In the 16th century European explorers venturing into the Amazon jungle were fascinated by

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.920
tales of a huge river monster.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:48.360
It was said to devour cattle and deer and to spit out water like shot from a cannon

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.920
knocking animals out of trees.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:59.600
These fantastic stories led people to go in search of this marvelous beast.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:07.760
In 1907 a British explorer Colonel Percy Fawcett claimed to have encountered an enormous snake

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.760
on the Amazon River.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:17.320
A huge head he said rose up from the water dangerously close to his canoe and a colossal

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.200
anaconda emerged.

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:24.280
Quickly alarmed he shot the snake dead.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:31.800
He claimed that when measured it proved to be nearly 19 meters over 60 feet long but

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:38.060
Fawcett's account was met with disbelief and he never provided convincing proof because

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:46.440
soon after that he vanished into the Brazilian jungle and was never seen again.

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:52.080
The creature that Fawcett encountered was almost certainly a green anaconda.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:57.520
Despite their massive proportions these huge snakes are seldom seen because they spend

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:04.660
most of their time in water waiting in ambush for their prey.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:10.700
In this murky world they're certainly well camouflaged and so some people believed that

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:19.920
somewhere another real monster might still be lurking unseen.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:27.260
In the 1960s a snake was brought to the Museum of Zoology at the University College London.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:28.680
This is it.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:34.620
It had lived in London Zoo for some years before it died and it was five meters long.

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:37.320
A lot of work went into preparing the skeleton.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:42.440
It had to be carried out onto the flat roof of the museum and it was finally displayed

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:50.840
in this rather unusual way wrapped around the branch of a tree.

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:58.880
For years the museum displayed it as an anaconda but in 2012 a member of the public saw an

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.560
old photo of the snake on the museum's website and pointed out that it looked like an African

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.920
rock python and not an anaconda.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.240
It's unclear how the mistake came about.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:18.360
The markings on the two snakes are quite different but both are giants and there's much controversy

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:22.280
as to which species is the largest snake of all.

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:27.400
Anacondas, pythons and boas like this one don't kill with venom.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:29.120
They're constrictors.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:35.880
They squeeze their prey to death and their coils can exert a very strong pressure indeed

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.800
as I can feel with this one on my arm.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:45.520
But a big anaconda can squeeze with the force of around 4,000 kilos.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:52.000
That's like having a bus on your chest and that can certainly crush the spine of a deer

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.680
or a capybara.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.160
And yet constrictor snakes don't usually crush their prey.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:06.800
In most cases they simply squeeze it so hard that the animal can't breathe.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:13.480
Every time its prey tries to inhale the snake's powerful muscles squeeze harder.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:30.600
The unfortunate victim then either dies because its blood can no longer circulate or suffocates.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:37.120
An anaconda or a python can kill prey that is not only twice its own body size but many

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:40.020
times bigger than its head.

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:47.440
So how does it manage to swallow its victim whole?

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:54.280
Popular folklore has it that anacondas and pythons unhinge or dislocate their jaws to

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:56.460
swallow large prey.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.460
That is not true.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:04.680
They do however have the ability to open their mouths wider than most animals.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:11.600
Pythons and anacondas have this additional bone attached to the back of the jaws.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:17.720
This provides a double hinge at the joint and allows them to open their jaws extremely

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:22.080
wide both downwards and sideways.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:28.880
In addition the two sides of the lower jaw are not fused together but joined by an elastic

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:30.640
ligament.

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:36.680
This gives the jaws a lot of stretch and they can even move apart when the snake is swallowing

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:38.520
large prey.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:44.920
It also allows each side of the jaw to move independently of the other.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:50.400
When eating a meal, particularly one that is much larger than itself, the snake can

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:57.880
alternately move its jaws on either side of its head and walk its prey into its mouth

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:02.800
even while its victim is still alive.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:08.940
As the jaws open wide, the snake's elastic skin stretches.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:13.480
But the mobility of the skull comes with a price.

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:19.560
Many of the joints that in other snakes are solid have been replaced by mobile ones.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:23.440
So the skull has less crushing power.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:34.320
As a consequence, the snake has to use its entire body to overpower its prey.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:39.340
Getting large prey into the mouth is one problem, but how does a snake push it all the way down

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:44.760
the length of its body into its stomach?

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:50.280
This is a Burmese python and it hasn't fed for a long time.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:18:10.080
So I'm hoping to give it a little breakfast with a dead rat.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:17.600
Saliva from the salivary glands in the mouth has moistened the prey so it's easier to swallow.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:23.760
And now it's moving its jaws, drawing the rat farther down its throat until eventually

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:30.640
the muscles of the flanks take over, squeezing the prey and pushing against the ribs so that

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:37.320
it looks as though the stake is, as it were, crawling around the rat.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:46.000
And that will continue for some time as the prey is worked down into the snake's body

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:57.680
until eventually it reaches the stomach, which is around the middle here.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:04.040
Equally remarkable is what happens inside the snake.

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:11.240
After months of fasting, it has to restart its digestive system quickly.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:15.840
Within a day, some of the internal organs double in size.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:20.960
The heart expands, pumping greater volumes of blood around the body.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:26.840
And special cells in the lining of the stomach produce powerful enzymes that break down flesh

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:31.840
and bones.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:42.240
And when the prey is entirely digested, the python's organs return to normal again.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:49.680
Snakes and pythons are able to take in enormous meals in a single mouthful.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:56.480
But how do they then survive fasting for months on end?

00:19:56.480 --> 00:20:02.460
Like all cold-blooded animals, snakes get much of their heat from the sun, so they need

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:08.160
less food to fuel their bodies and most of what they eat is converted directly into body

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:11.480
mass.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:17.280
Snakes continue to grow throughout their lives, and anacondas get bigger than any other species

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.520
because they live mostly in water.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:26.480
Their massive bodies supported by its buoyancy.

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:32.640
So it's certainly possible that an anaconda could grow to an enormous size.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:41.280
But how large can a snake really get?

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:48.400
In 2009, further light was shed on this question with the discovery of the fossils of a super

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:49.600
snake.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:56.840
It was given the name Titanoboa, and it suggests that snakes can get very large indeed.

00:20:56.840 --> 00:21:04.000
Titanoboa was nearly 13 meters long, the length of a bus, and must have weighed over a ton.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:09.360
It lived around 60 million years ago, shortly after the extinction of the dinosaurs.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:13.800
We don't know for sure, but it may be that the warmer climate of the earth at the time

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:18.480
allowed cold-blooded snakes to grow much larger in size.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:25.000
What is certain is that for at least 10 million years, Titanoboa was the largest predator

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:29.880
on the planet.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:36.560
Both the camel and the anaconda can withstand extreme periods of fasting.

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:42.200
But it's only by looking inside the camel's hump and the anaconda's stomach that we've

00:21:42.200 --> 00:22:11.960
discovered the truth behind their amazing expandable bodies.
